module iefFocusingDetection
module cgeDetection

manifest Ampholyte_3_10
manifest GlycerolMethylcellulose
manifest GFP_Sample
manifest OvalSample
manifest LDLPSample
manifest TrypsinInhibitorSample
manifest AcrylamideCoating
manifest AnolyteSolution
manifest CatholyteSolution
manifest CGE_GelBuffer
manifest CZE_Buffer

// ============================================================
// Two-Dimensional Protein Separation with Advanced Sample and
// Buffer Isolation Using Microfluidic Valves
// Wang, Choi, Han - Anal. Chem. 2004, 76, 4426-4431
// DOI: 10.1021/ac0497499
//
// Platform: Double-layer PDMS microfluidic chip with
// pneumatic monolith valves (Unger et al., Science 2000).
// Separation channels: 150 um wide, 12 um high. IEF channel:
// 10 mm. CE channel: 25 mm. Peak transfer region: 3 mm.
// Control channels: 300 um wide, 50 um high (5 mm thick PDMS).
// Substrate: poly(dimethylsiloxane) (PDMS) on flat glass.
//
// Objective: Integrate IEF (charge-based, 1st dimension) with
// CGE or CZE (size/charge-based, 2nd dimension) using active
// microvalve control to prevent buffer interdiffusion between
// incompatible separation media (ampholyte vs. CE buffer).
//
// Samples (labeled with fluorescein or Alexa Fluor 488):
//   GFP            (pI 5.6, MW 26 kDa)
//   Ovalbumin      (pI 5.1, MW 45 kDa)
//   LDL Protein    (pI 5.11, MW 179 kDa)
//   Trypsin Inhib. (pI 4.6, MW 20 kDa)
// IEF sample: 3-10 ampholyte + 1% methylcellulose + 10%
// glycerol + aqueous labeled proteins.
//
// This file encodes the IEF-CGE 2D separation arm (Figure 3/4
// in the paper) as the primary representative experiment.
// The IEF-CZE arm follows an identical four-step procedure
// with CGE_GelBuffer replaced by CZE_Buffer and a 250 V/cm
// field in the second dimension; it is noted in comments below
// but not separately encoded. See paper Section "IEF Coupled
// with Second-Dimension Capillary Zone Electrophoresis."
//
// NOTE ON PLATFORM COMPATIBILITY: This is a continuous-flow
// pressure-driven + electrokinetic chip, not a DMF/EWOD device.
// BioScript `mix` abstracts pressure-driven injection and
// electrokinetic buffer loading steps. `heat` abstracts
// electric field application (IEF focusing, CGE/CE separation)
// because BioScript has no native electrokinetic primitive.
// `detect` abstracts fluorescence microscopy readout
// (inverted epi-fluorescence, FITC filter, CCD camera,
// IPLab 3.6 at 1 frame/s).
//
// NOTE ON SURFACE PASSIVATION: The chip requires a static
// polyacrylamide coating (via 3-(trimethoxysilyl)propyl
// methacrylate adhesion promoter) followed by dynamic 0.5%
// methylcellulose coating before use. This is modelled as a
// dispense + heat step at 25 C for 30 min (polymerization
// under N2 purge in vacuum chamber). This is a one-time chip
// preparation step; it does not consume a droplet in the
// electrophoretic sense, but must appear before loading to
// satisfy fluid variable ownership rules.
//
// NOTE ON VALVE ACTUATION: Pneumatic valves are controlled by
// 80 psi pressure on the top control layer. Closing right
// valves (3,4) isolates the IEF channel from the CGE channel;
// closing left valves (1,2) traps the peak transfer region.
// BioScript has no native valve primitive; valve state changes
// are abstracted as mix steps transitioning between separation
// phases (loading, isolation, transfer). Dead volume at peak
// transfer region: 0.1 nL (smaller than commercial valves).
//
// NOTE ON h DURATION TOKEN: BioScript (lilott8 compiler)
// supports only `s` and `m` duration tokens. All durations
// are expressed in seconds (s) or minutes (m).
//
// CFG STRUCTURE:
//   BB1  main            -- chip prep, IEF buffer loading,
//                           sample loading, IEF focusing,
//                           peak isolation by valve closure
//   BB2  iefPass         -- iefSignal > 0: focused peaks
//                           detected in transfer region;
//                           dispose and proceed to CGE
//   BB3  iefFail         -- iefSignal <= 0: focusing failed
//                           (EOF too high or no peaks); drain
//   BB4  iefJoin         -- join: CGE separation branch begins
//   BB5  cgePass         -- cgeSignal > 0: proteins separated
//                           in 2nd dimension; dispose all
//   BB6  cgeFail         -- cgeSignal <= 0: no CGE signal; drain
//   BB7  finalCleanup    -- terminal: dispose QC signals
// ============================================================

instructions:

// ============================================================
// BB1: main
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 1: Surface passivation - static polyacrylamide coating.
//   Coat PDMS channel with 3-(trimethoxysilyl)propyl
//   methacrylate adhesion promoter, then introduce acrylamide
//   + TEMED + APS solution under N2 purge (5 psi vacuum) for
//   30 min at 25 C to grow polyacrylamide polymer on surface.
//   Followed by 0.5% methylcellulose dynamic coating.
//   Modelled as heat at 25 C for 30 min (polymerization step).
//   `mix` abstracts the introduction of coating solution into
//   the microfluidic channel via pressure-driven flow.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

coatingBase   = dispense 1 units of AcrylamideCoating
viscosityAdd  = dispense 1 units of GlycerolMethylcellulose
coatingMix    = mix coatingBase with viscosityAdd for 5s

// Polymerization under N2 purge, 25 C, 30 min.
heat coatingMix at 25c for 30m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 2: Load CGE gel buffer into the second-dimension channel.
//   Right valves (3, 4) are closed first (80 psi) to isolate
//   the IEF channel. CGE gel buffer: SDS 14-200 (90% SDS
//   14-200 gel buffer + 1% methylcellulose) injected at
//   20-30 psi into the CE channel without contaminating the
//   IEF channel. Up to 60 psi can be applied without
//   breaking valve seal. Modelled as dispense + mix
//   abstracting pressure-driven injection into the CE channel.
//   (Paper Figure 1b: CE media loading step)
// ------------------------------------------------------------

cgeGelBuffer  = dispense 1 units of CGE_GelBuffer
gelLoadedChannel = mix coatingMix with cgeGelBuffer for 10s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 3: Prepare IEF sample mixture.
//   IEF sample: 3-10 ampholyte (Beckman Coulter) + 10%
//   glycerol + 1% methylcellulose + labeled protein samples.
//   1% methylcellulose suppresses electroosmotic flow (EOF)
//   to keep focused peaks stable in the 10 mm IEF channel.
//   Four proteins loaded simultaneously.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

ampholyteMix  = dispense 1 units of Ampholyte_3_10
glycerolMC    = dispense 1 units of GlycerolMethylcellulose

iefBase       = mix ampholyteMix with glycerolMC for 5s

gfpSample     = dispense 1 units of GFP_Sample
ovalSample    = dispense 1 units of OvalSample
ldlpSample    = dispense 1 units of LDLPSample
tiSample      = dispense 1 units of TrypsinInhibitorSample

// Combine all four labeled proteins into the ampholyte carrier.
sampleMix1    = mix gfpSample with ovalSample for 5s
sampleMix2    = mix ldlpSample with tiSample for 5s
proteinPool   = mix sampleMix1 with sampleMix2 for 5s

iefSample     = mix iefBase with proteinPool for 10s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 4: Load IEF sample + replace reservoir buffers.
//   Left valves (1, 2) closed (80 psi) to isolate CGE gel.
//   Right valves (3, 4) opened. Ampholyte-sample mixture
//   injected into the 10 mm IEF channel at low pressure.
//   Reservoir solutions then replaced: anolyte (0.1 M acetic
//   acid, pH 2.5) at anode end; catholyte (0.5% ammonium
//   hydroxide, pH 10.5) at cathode end.
//   (Paper Figure 1c: IEF ampholyte mixture loading step)
// ------------------------------------------------------------

anolyte       = dispense 1 units of AnolyteSolution
catholyte     = dispense 1 units of CatholyteSolution

// Buffer replacement at reservoirs modelled as mix.
anolyteLoad   = mix iefSample with anolyte for 5s
iefChannel    = mix anolyteLoad with catholyte for 5s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 5: IEF focusing (1st dimension).
//   500 V/cm applied across 10 mm IEF channel (total: 500 V).
//   Proteins focus to stationary bands at their pI under the
//   pH 3-10 gradient. Focusing equilibration: < 30 s (2 orders
//   of magnitude faster than CIEF in 25-50 cm capillaries).
//   EOF mobilizes focused peaks slowly from anode to cathode.
//   IEF steady state confirmed by monitoring current.
//   Modelled as heat at 37 C for 130 s (electric field applied
//   130 s per paper Figure 3a caption; temperature abstraction
//   encodes field-driven step duration).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

heat iefChannel at 37c for 130s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 6: Fluorescence detection - IEF peak verification.
//   Inverted epi-fluorescence microscope (Olympus, FITC filter,
//   100 W Hg lamp). CCD camera (Cooke Co., 1 frame/s).
//   Verify that protein peaks (GFP pI 5.6, Oval pI 5.1,
//   LDLP pI 5.11, TI pI 4.6) are resolved in IEF channel
//   and have entered the 3 mm peak transfer region.
//   iefFocusingDetection module abstracts fluorescence imaging
//   and peak position verification.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

iefSignal = detect iefFocusingDetection on iefChannel for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB2 / BB3: IEF focusing QC gate
// Pass (BB2): iefSignal > 0 -> peaks detected in transfer
//             region; proceed to peak isolation and CGE.
// Fail (BB3): iefSignal <= 0 -> focusing failed (EOF too
//             high, no gradient, or no peaks in transfer
//             region); drain channel contents.
// Both branches converge at BB4 (iefJoin).
// ============================================================

if (iefSignal > 0) {
    dispose iefChannel
} else {
    drain iefChannel
}

// ============================================================
// BB4: iefJoin
// Dispose IEF QC signal. Control falls into CGE separation
// branch (BB5/BB6). Peak transfer region now holds isolated
// protein band(s) trapped by closed valve sets.
// ============================================================

dispose iefSignal

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 7: Peak isolation by valve closure.
//   After 130 s of IEF focusing, target protein peaks have
//   entered the 3 mm peak transfer region. Both valve sets
//   (1,2 and 3,4) are closed at 80 psi to physically isolate
//   the peak transfer region from both the IEF channel and
//   the CGE channel. Isolation resistance: > 1000 MOhm at
//   100 psi; no protein intercontamination observed after
//   30 min. Dead volume: 0.1 nL.
//   Modelled as dispense of isolated peak region (re-entry
//   of isolated fluid into BioScript variable scope).
//   (Paper Figure 1d: isolation of target proteins)
// ------------------------------------------------------------

isolatedPeaks = dispense 1 units of GFP_Sample

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 8: Buffer equilibration before CGE field application.
//   Left valves (1, 2) opened. CGE buffer ions allowed to
//   diffuse into the peak transfer region for 30 s to prevent
//   abrupt conductivity jump at the buffer boundary. Protein
//   diffusion is slow (large molecules) relative to buffer
//   ion diffusion; this step does not degrade IEF resolution.
//   Small-MW ampholyte molecules co-isolated with proteins
//   will be rapidly eluted by electrophoresis (lower MW).
//   (Paper Figure 1e and 3b: 30 s equilibration period)
// ------------------------------------------------------------

cgeEquil     = dispense 1 units of CGE_GelBuffer
equilibrated = mix isolatedPeaks with cgeEquil for 30s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 9: CGE separation (2nd dimension).
//   250 V/cm applied across the 25 mm CE channel (total:
//   625 V). Proteins entering the high-conductivity CGE gel
//   buffer from the low-conductivity ampholyte stacking zone
//   experience sample stacking at the boundary: sudden
//   decrease in electrophoretic velocity creates a band-
//   narrowing effect. Stacking makes 1st and 2nd dimension
//   separations independent (Figure 5, paper).
//   Separation basis: charge-to-mass ratio (non-denaturing
//   CGE, no SDS). Ovalbumin (45 kDa) has higher mobility
//   than GFP (26 kDa) due to higher charge-to-mass ratio
//   (ovalbumin mu = -1.3e4 cm2/V.s; GFP mu = -1.0e4 cm2/V.s).
//   Full 2D separation achieved within 10 min total.
//   Modelled as heat at 37 C for 6m (250 V/cm for ~6 min
//   per Figure 4b electropherogram time axis).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

heat equilibrated at 37c for 6m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 10: Fluorescence detection - CGE readout.
//   Electropherograms acquired at end of 25 mm CGE channel
//   by fluorescence intensity measurement. FITC filter set.
//   CCD camera, 1 frame/s, IPLab 3.6 acquisition.
//   cgeDetection module abstracts fluorescence electropherogram
//   acquisition and peak identification.
//   Expected elution order: Ovalbumin elutes before GFP
//   (higher charge-to-mass ratio, faster mobility).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

cgeSignal = detect cgeDetection on equilibrated for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB5 / BB6: CGE separation QC gate
// Pass (BB5): cgeSignal > 0 -> protein peaks detected in
//             2nd-dimension electropherogram; dispose.
// Fail (BB6): cgeSignal <= 0 -> no CGE signal (stacking
//             failed, valve leak, or detector issue); drain.
// Both branches converge at BB7 (finalCleanup).
// ============================================================

if (cgeSignal > 0) {
    dispose equilibrated
} else {
    drain equilibrated
}

// ============================================================
// BB7: finalCleanup (terminal)
// Dispose CGE QC signal droplet. Experiment ends.
// Total protocol time: ~10 min (per paper conclusion).
//
// NOTE ON IEF-CZE ARM: The paper also demonstrates IEF
// coupled with capillary zone electrophoresis (CZE/CE) using
// CZE_Buffer (0.375 M Tris-Cl, pH 8.6, 0.1% methylcellulose)
// instead of CGE_GelBuffer, and 250 V/cm in the 2nd dimension.
// The stacking mechanism still operates (liquid-liquid
// conductivity boundary). The CZE arm is structurally
// identical to the CGE arm above with reagent substitution.
// A separate .bs file should encode the IEF-CZE arm with
// CZE_Buffer substituted for CGE_GelBuffer at Steps 2 and 8.
// ============================================================

dispose cgeSignal

// [terminal -- no further instructions; exit node for CFG]
